         sub remove_messages {
             my $self = shift;

             I: for (my $i = 0; $i < @{ $self->queued_messages }; ) {
                 next if @{ $self->queued_messages->[$i] } < @_;
                 for (my $j = 0; $j < @_; ++$j) {
                     if ($self->queued_messages->[$i][$j] ne $_[$j]) {
                         ++$i;
                         next I;
                     }
                 }
                 splice @{ $self->queued_messages }, $i, 1;
             }
         }


Gods that's ugly code, and I'm not just talking
about the barely disguised GOTO in there.

The line starting "next if", for example, contains,
count them, _16_ puncuation characters compared to
25 alphanumeric characters.

That's _worse_ that the non-whitespace 22::62 ratio
of punctuation to alphanumeric characters in the
ASCII alphabet, so it looks more like line noise
even than line noise does.

The punctuation characters are needed because that
is how PERL syntax is defined, and how PERL syntax
is defined is as very, very slightly modified line
noise.

