I was not intending to start a war here.

I thought I was stating the well known, frequently
mentioned, and profoundly obvious characteristic of
PERL, its lack of readability.

Last time I checked the most broken single
characteristic of PERL was that it wasn't free
format, like even C is.

If one doesn't follow the ugly usual-C-style
opening-bracket-at-end-of-line layout, and does
something sane instead, like stacking opening and
closing brackets vertically to make code easy to
parse by eye, that code stops working.

That is

   example {
   }

works, versus

   example
   {
   }

which does, or used to, break in some cases.

This might well have been fixed since whenever,
though. I don't do much exploring in PERL any more.

Much like Java has done, it encrusted itself with so
much baggage disguising itself as "standard" or
"need to know", no single user can learn it all any
more.

 > Shawn

What does recommend PERL as a replacement language
for C in rewriting NetHack?

1) Its an OOPL, a hands down win.

2) It has a more active, bigger, hacker-mentality
community available from which to recruit coders.

3) It has a _huge_ freely accessible, well
organized (well, maybe, it's plenty easy to get lost
there) module code base, CPAN.

4) It has a much more powerful regular expression
mechanism built-in than C provides as library code.

5) Its native data structures are much richer than
C's, more easily able to support NetHack's complex
data types in ways that make them much easier to
extend (like hashes).

6) Its module code gets banged on harder and thus
debugged faster, than NetHack's existing code base.

7) It was designed by someone who rejected the
concept of there being only "One True Way" to do
anything, and gave the programmer lots of options.

8) It has excellent documentation written by the
same someone, who _likes_ programmers instead of
barely tolerating them.

9) It's execution speed is plenty fast enough for a
finger-speed typing game.

